RHEL OS UPGRADE PERFORMANCE REPORTING — v2.0
Standalone same-account Python Lambda | 3 invocations -> 4 standard HTML reports
Last reviewed: 2026-11-17

WHAT CHANGED IN V2
==================
- Works with the CloudWatch Agent configuration you supplied, including the
  renamed metric names: CpuUserPercent, CpuSystemPercent, CpuIOWaitPercent,
  CpuIdlePercent, MemUsedPercent, MemAvailableBytes, MemAvailablePercent,
  DiskUsedPercent, DiskTotalBytes, DiskUsedBytes, DiskFreeBytes,
  SwapUsedBytes, SwapFreeBytes, SwapUsedPercent, NetBytesIn, NetBytesOut.
- Also reads the configured unrenamed metrics: diskio_reads/writes,
  diskio_read_time/write_time/io_time, net_drop_in/out, and netstat_*.
- Selects per-device/per-filesystem/per-interface metrics where appropriate.
- Phase N generates phase-N.html AND phase-N-status.json under one S3 batch path.
- Phase 3 ALSO generates the final-summary.html (four standard HTML reports).
- The phase-N-status.json provides software version, OS version, SSM status,
  metrics available, expected hours, WARN/FAIL findings, and comparisons.
- FATAL phase invocation problems generate best-effort timestamped error .html
  and .json diagnostics without replacing earlier successful phase HTML.
- No OS changes; SSM executes a read-only inspection script.

REPLACE THESE PLACEHOLDERS
==========================
ACCOUNT_ID      = 12-digit AWS account ID
REGION          = same AWS region as the EC2 instances (e.g. ap-southeast-2)
BUCKET_NAME     = existing S3 report bucket
EXISTING_PREFIX = chosen existing bucket prefix without leading/trailing slash
KMS_KEY_ARN     = ARN of customer-managed key protecting the S3 objects
ROLE_NAME       = RhelOsUpgradeReportLambdaRole

1. LAMBDA EXECUTION ROLE — IAM
==============================
AWS Console -> IAM -> Roles -> Create role -> AWS service -> Lambda.

Trust policy:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"},
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
}

Attach AWS-managed: AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole

Add this INLINE policy, replacing every placeholder:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "ReadEC2InstancesAndEBSVolumes",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["ec2:DescribeInstances", "ec2:DescribeVolumes"],
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "ReadEC2AndCWAgentMetrics",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["cloudwatch:ListMetrics", "cloudwatch:GetMetricData"],
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "ExecuteReadOnlyShellAuditWithSSM",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "ssm:SendCommand",
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:ssm:REGION::document/AWS-RunShellScript",
        "arn:aws:ec2:REGION:ACCOUNT_ID:instance/*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Sid": "GetSSMAuditResults",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "ssm:GetCommandInvocation",
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "ReadWriteEncryptedReportsUnderExistingPrefix",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject", "s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME/EXISTING_PREFIX/*"
    },
    {
      "Sid": "ListExistingReportBatchState",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:ListBucket",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::BUCKET_NAME",
      "Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": [
        "EXISTING_PREFIX", "EXISTING_PREFIX/*"
      ]}}
    },
    {
      "Sid": "UseExistingCustomerManagedKmsKeyViaS3",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["kms:GenerateDataKey", "kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "KMS_KEY_ARN",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {
        "kms:ViaService": "s3.REGION.amazonaws.com"
      }}
    }
  ]
}

IAM SECURITY NOTE
-----------------
ssm:SendCommand with AWS-RunShellScript is a powerful permission even when this
Lambda only sends a read-only command. Scope the EC2 instance resource to a
well-defined inventory/tag or use a dedicated SSM document for tighter control.
The function does not require CloudWatch Logs read permissions; it only queries
CloudWatch METRICS and takes a limited current-boot journal snapshot via SSM.
AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole writes *Lambda function* execution logs.

2. EXISTING S3 CUSTOMER-MANAGED KMS KEY POLICY
===============================================
The Lambda role must be able to write and read S3 objects encrypted with this
key. If the key policy does not delegate access to IAM for this account,
request the KMS key owner to ADD the following statement to the EXISTING key
policy (never replace the existing key admin/recovery statements):
{
  "Sid": "AllowRhelOsUpgradeReportingLambdaViaS3",
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": {
    "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/ROLE_NAME"
  },
  "Action": ["kms:GenerateDataKey", "kms:Decrypt"],
  "Resource": "*",
  "Condition": {"StringEquals": {
    "kms:ViaService": "s3.REGION.amazonaws.com"
  }}
}

If the S3 bucket policy has an explicit Deny or restricts encryption-key ARNs
or encryption contexts, update/check it separately with your security team.
Use the same-region KMS key required by your bucket policy.
Your own IAM principal also needs s3:GetObject and kms:Decrypt to download the
reports. A role with access only to the bucket will NOT automatically be able
to decrypt SSE-KMS objects.

3. EC2 SIDE PREREQUISITES
========================
- Instances running, Name tag unique and matching event JSON exactly.
- SSM managed node Online; EC2 instance profile with AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore.
- CloudWatch Agent installed, working, publishing in namespace CWAgent.
- Existing CloudWatch Agent config supplied by you has:
     append_dimensions: InstanceId, ImageId, InstanceType
     aggregation_dimensions: [InstanceId], [ImageId,InstanceType]
- Agent metrics are case-sensitive. V2 looks for renamed metrics as supplied.
- No changes to the existing CWAgent configuration are required for the metrics
  listed above. HOWEVER, unconfigured guest metrics (e.g. diskio_read_bytes,
  diskio_write_bytes, net_err_in/out, disk_inodes_free) cannot be reconstructed
  historically. Inodes are checked from live SSM snapshots, and EBS throughput
  is read from AWS/EBS metrics if published.
- Your CloudWatch log groups are already receiving logs (as configured):
     /prod/linux/cw-agent
     equip-cw-ec2-system-messages-logs
     equip-cw-ec2-system-secure-logs
  This version deliberately DOES NOT scan those log groups. It collects a
  limited current-boot journal/kernel error excerpt from SSM at each phase.
- To check actual published metrics manually: CloudWatch -> Metrics ->
  All metrics -> CWAgent -> InstanceId -> choose an instance's renamed metric.

4. DEPLOY AND CONFIGURE LAMBDA
==============================
1. Open AWS account and REGION containing the target EC2 instances.
2. Lambda -> Create function -> Author from scratch.
   Name: RhelOsUpgradePerformanceReport
   Runtime: Python 3.12
   Execution role: the role created above
   Timeout: 15 min. Memory: initially 1024 MB.
3. Upload the ZIP containing ONLY rhel_os_upgrade_report_lambda.py at ZIP root,
   or paste that file's contents into the Lambda editor and click Deploy.
   Handler must be:
      rhel_os_upgrade_report_lambda.lambda_handler
4. Lambda -> Configuration -> Environment variables:
      REPORT_BUCKET=BUCKET_NAME
      REPORT_PREFIX=EXISTING_PREFIX
      KMS_KEY_ARN=KMS_KEY_ARN
      CW_AGENT_NAMESPACE=CWAgent       [optional; default CWAgent]
      BASELINE_DAYS=15                [optional]
      SSM_WAIT_SECONDS=100           [optional]
      MAX_HOSTS=20                   [optional]
5. Lambda -> Test -> Create test event -> paste the JSON below.
6. Verify in the Lambda RETURN JSON:
      script_version: "2026-11-17-v2.0"
      report: "s3://.../phase-1.html"
      status_json: "s3://.../phase-1-status.json"
      execution_status: COMPLETED_PASS | COMPLETED_WITH_WARNINGS |
                        COMPLETED_WITH_FAILURES
   A returned COMPLETED_WITH_FAILURES means Lambda finished writing the HTML,
   but one or more server checks FAILED. It is not a Lambda execution crash.
   Check CloudWatch -> Lambda -> Monitor -> View CloudWatch Logs for crashes.
7. For S3 HTML, select the .html object in S3 -> Download -> open the local
   .html file in a browser. The CSV buttons work offline. S3 Console Open may
   download an HTML object rather than render it; that is normal.

If the Lambda is attached to a VPC it needs NAT or suitable endpoints for the
AWS services it calls (EC2, CloudWatch Monitoring, SSM, S3, STS as relevant).
For normal operation, the Lambda does NOT need to be attached to the EC2 VPC.

5. HOW TO INVOKE ALL THREE PHASES
=================================
Run 1 BEFORE first upgrade (Name-tag values are supplied HERE):
{
  "phase": 1,
  "batch_id": "nov26-wave01",
  "hostnames": ["APP-SERVER-01", "DB-SERVER-01"],
  "server_types": {"APP-SERVER-01": "app", "DB-SERVER-01": "db"},
  "critical_services_by_host": {
    "APP-SERVER-01": ["sshd", "chronyd"],
    "DB-SERVER-01": ["sshd", "chronyd"]
  },
  "baseline_days": 15
}

Run 2 AFTER the first upgrade (ONE UTC completion timestamp PER hostname):
{
  "phase": 2,
  "batch_id": "nov26-wave01",
  "upgrade_completed_at_utc": {
    "APP-SERVER-01": "2026-11-20T03:30:00Z",
    "DB-SERVER-01": "2026-11-21T05:15:00Z"
  }
}

Run 3 AFTER final upgrade (again give completion time for EACH server):
{
  "phase": 3,
  "batch_id": "nov26-wave01",
  "upgrade_completed_at_utc": {
    "APP-SERVER-01": "2026-12-02T04:15:00Z",
    "DB-SERVER-01": "2026-12-03T02:45:00Z"
  }
}

The events for runs 2 and 3 DO NOT need to repeat hostnames. Each run looks
up Phase 1 state by matching batch_id, and checks that the InstanceId still
matches the Name tag in the account. Expected OS releases:
  APP: 8.8 -> 9.2 -> 9.6
  DB : 8.8 -> 8.10 -> 9.6
Verify your actual Red Hat vendor-supported upgrade sequence separately.
Lambda only REPORTS status; it NEVER upgrades or reboots the EC2 instances.

IMPORTANT TIMING: Lambda cannot reliably infer when an in-place OS upgrade
finished just from the last reboot or EC2 launch time. Specify the actual UTC
completion time from your change record. Each post-upgrade metric window starts
at the FIRST COMPLETE HOUR after that timestamp and ends at the last full
hour before the Lambda invocation. If you run immediately after an upgrade,
the HTML still generates, but the report flags missing/insufficient data.
Recommend at least 24 hours, ideally 5-7 days of typical workload, before
accepting performance comparisons.

6. EXACT S3 OUTPUT TREE
=======================
s3://BUCKET_NAME/EXISTING_PREFIX/nov26-wave01/
    phase-1.html              [HTML baseline validation; 15 days metrics]
    phase-1-status.json       [quick phase 1 run/health diagnostic]
    phase-2.html              [HTML Phase 2; delta vs Phase 1; OS checks]
    phase-2-status.json       [quick phase 2 run/health diagnostic]
    phase-3.html              [HTML Phase 3; delta vs Phase 2; OS checks]
    phase-3-status.json       [quick phase 3 run/health diagnostic]
    final-summary.html        [full three-phase comparison, generated Run 3]
    .state/phase-1.json       [INTERNAL complete metric data, NOT a report]
    .state/phase-2.json
    .state/phase-3.json

This is FOUR STANDARD HTML REPORTS, plus diagnostic JSON and internal state.
All S3 objects are uploaded with aws:kms + the configured KMS_KEY_ARN.
If a Lambda call fails before a normal report, a best-effort diagnostic pair
named phase-N-execution-error-YYYYMMDDTHHMMSS*.json/.html will be written.
It may not be written if the S3 bucket/KMS/IAM permission itself is the reason
for failure. In that case, use the Lambda CloudWatch execution logs.

7. WHAT IS INSIDE EACH HTML REPORT?
===================================
Each individual phase HTML has:
- Batch/account/region/script_version, per-host OS version, instance ID/type.
- Start/end of metric window (UTC); number of complete hourly bins.
- Top-level PASS/WARN/FAIL summary and prioritized ACTION REQUIRED table.
- For Phase 2 / 3: before vs after metric table with relative percentage delta,
  plus PASS/WARN flags for unfavorable CPU/memory/disk/network deviations.
- Guest CPU user/system/idle/iowait, memory, swap, filesystems, disk reads,
  writes, read/write guest latencies, network bytes, drops, TCP states where
  data is available; AWS/EC2 CPU/status/network and AWS/EBS disk performance.
- All service, fstab, file mount, inode, SELinux, DNS, NTP, journal and kernel
  checks available from an SSM snapshot during each phase.
- All metric rows show hourly MEAN, P95 of hourly values, min, max, samples,
  coverage and a trend sparkline. Missing series are NOT treated as zero.
- CSV export buttons for summary metrics, hourly metrics, validations.

Phase 3 also builds final-summary.html, covering:
- Every instance's three OS versions, final PASS/WARN/FAIL, exceptions.
- Every supported metric's hourly average at each stage and % changes:
  Phase 1 -> Phase 2, Phase 2 -> Phase 3, Phase 1 -> Phase 3.
- OS configuration and service comparison across all three phases.
- Full individual OS validation evidence for each phase; CSV export for
  all comparisons, validations, and metrics.

Phase-N-status.json provides short machine-readable diagnostics:
  script_version, execution_status, phase, date, html_s3_key, host_statuses,
  detected/expected RHEL version, SSM status, metric collection window, count
  of discovered agent metrics, metrics actually containing points, all WARN/
  FAIL findings and flagged deviations since the previous upgrade phase.
This file is not a substitute for the detailed HTML report.

8. METRIC COVERAGE AND IMPORTANT LIMITATIONS
============================================
- CloudWatch metric namespace and names are case-sensitive. The supplied
  configuration uses CWAgent and the renamed values above.
- Historical performance cannot be reconstructed for measurements that were
  never published. The Lambda does not modify agent configuration.
- diskio read_bytes/write_bytes, net_err_in/out and historical inode usage
  are not in the supplied agent config. EBS byte throughput is reported from
  AWS/EBS when available; live inode usage is captured by SSM.
- The agent logs /prod/linux/cw-agent, /var/log/messages and /var/log/secure*
  are not queried. Limited recent log evidence comes from current-boot SSM.
- Status-check failures and high disk usage are FAIL/WARN; snapshot or
  necessary service failures are FAIL; missing configured metrics are WARN.
- Example performance regression WARN thresholds: CPU +15%, memory +10%,
  other unfavorable directional metrics +20% relative to comparison window.
  This is screening evidence, not proof the OS caused the change. Compare
  matching workload periods before formally accepting the upgrade.
- Completed hourly CloudWatch metrics are retained up to 455 days by AWS;
  the baseline window uses 15 days. Agent metrics from 60/120-second samples
  are aggregated into hourly report bins.
- CloudWatch ListMetrics does not return metrics inactive for the last ~2
  weeks. If retired metrics do not appear, they will be reported as missing.
- The original phase data is saved in S3 for later comparisons. NEVER delete
  the .state folder before Phase 3.
- Use a NEW batch_id for each upgrade wave. Runs must be sequential, not
  overlapping. An existing completed phase will be blocked from overwrite by
  default. To intentionally rerun Phase 2 before Phase 3 or Phase 3 itself,
  set "replace_existing": true. After Phase 3, Phase 2 can no longer be
  overwritten. Prefer fresh batch IDs for tests.

9. TROUBLESHOOTING
=================
Only JSON, no phase HTML visible:
  - Check the S3 path used by Lambda, including /<batch_id>/.
  - .state/phase-N.json is INTERNAL and not the report.
  - Check script_version in Lambda result or phase-N-status.json; it MUST be
    2026-11-17-v2.0 if you deployed this updated file.
  - Check Lambda handler points to rhel_os_upgrade_report_lambda.lambda_handler,
    save code changes, click Deploy, and run with a NEW batch_id.
  - Check Lambda execution logs for S3 PutObject error and KMS permission.

No memory / disk / swap / guest network data:
  - Check CWAgent metrics by InstanceId and exact renamed metric names.
  - Check the metric has 1-hour points in the requested time range.
  - Check CW_AGENT_NAMESPACE=CWAgent.
  - Review phase-N-status.json's guest_metrics_with_data, and the HTML
    ACTION REQUIRED section; look for 'Historical metric missing'.

Phase 2 version mismatch (e.g. DB still 8.8):
  - Lambda reports FAIL for expected RHEL release and highlights the current
    OS version and required version in phase-2.html and phase-2-status.json.
  - Fix upgrade issue with your OS team before proceeding; rerun Phase 2 with
    replace_existing=true if it is safe and Phase 3 has not yet been run.
  - If rebuilding/replacing EC2 instance changed InstanceId, use new batch.

Phase 2 has no historical hourly points:
  - You may have invoked too soon after upgrade. Wait until enough complete
    hourly points are in CloudWatch and rerun Phase 2 deliberately.
  - Confirm the supplied timestamp is accurate in UTC and follows the
    preceding phase's report time.

Fatal invocation error:
  - Check Lambda Test result and CloudWatch Lambda logs.
  - The function tries to write phase-N-execution-error-<timestamp>.html and
    .json. This cannot work if its S3 permissions or KMS key are broken.
